+    *   Guarantees reversibility (OBQ property) and topological correctness [S1].
+    *   Significant reduction in facet count (often >75%) [S1].
+    *   Local analysis suffices for ensuring properties, avoiding complex global topology checks [S1].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Restricted to simple MC configurations for global optimization; complex configurations are not optimized [S1].
+    *   Visual quality may vary compared to other simplification methods, as the algorithm prioritizes reversibility over aesthetic smoothness [S1].
+    *   Bottleneck in DPS recognition for large volumes due to rational arithmetic [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When reversible polyhedrization of binary volumes is required, particularly in medical imaging or digital geometry applications where the exact voxel boundary must be preserved [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When visual smoothness is the primary concern and reversibility is not required, or when processing very large volumes where rational arithmetic overhead is prohibitive [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed by David Coeurjolly, Florent Dupont, and Isabelle Sivignon from LIRIS, CNRS, France [S1]. It is based on the Object Boundary Quantization (OBQ) model, which establishes that the Marching Cubes surface with an iso-level in $]0, 1[$ is a reversible polyhedrization of a binary object [S1]. The work builds on previous research in digital plane segmentation and discrete geometry [S1, S2, S3, S4, S10, S13, S14, S15, S16, S17]. The algorithm was presented in the context of optimizing reversible discrete volume polyhedrization [S1].
